 ## Overview
-Scalable Distributed Volume Rendering (SDVR) is a distributed rendering system for unstructured data targeting large PC clusters, using OpenGL and MPI, designed for scalability where rendering time remains constant as data size and node count increase [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering massive unstructured grids that exceed the memory or processing capacity of a single machine by partitioning the dataset into load-balanced chunks and distributing them across a cluster [S1][S2, unofficial]. What sets it apart from its siblings is its specific focus on unstructured data via an out-of-core modified k-d decomposition for partitioning, and its design to run with or without graphics hardware on any platform, although not at peak performance without it [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Scalable Distributed Volume Rendering (SDVR) is a distributed rendering system for unstructured data targeting large PC clusters, designed to maintain constant rendering time as dataset size and node count increase [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the scalability problem in volume visualization by partitioning the dataset into load-balanced chunks, distributing them to cluster nodes, and accumulating subimages over the interconnect [S1][S2, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [MPI Volume Rendering (1D/2D Decomposition)](2249-mpi-volume-rendering-1d-2d-decomposition.md) by using an out-of-core modified k-d decomposition for preprocessing and explicit subimage accumulation over the network rather than pixel-based accumulation or image-based assembly by a root process [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
